WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.554
A schema can reject a value only after

2
00:00:03.554 --> 00:00:06.798
enough input has been processed to
evaluate it.

3
00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:10.902
Large bodies, deep nesting, long strings,
and enormous collections can

4
00:00:10.902 --> 00:00:14.910
consume memory or processing time before a
business rule runs.

5
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.536
JSON parsing does not know the
application's acceptable workload.

6
00:00:18.536 --> 00:00:22.449
Resource protection therefore begins at
transport and parser boundaries, with

7
00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:26.457
measured limits suited to the operations
the service actually supports.

8
00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:29.607
Limit request and message bytes before
parsing, and

9
00:00:29.607 --> 00:00:33.233
account for decompressed size when
compressed input is accepted.

10
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.714
Bound strings, array lengths, object
property

11
00:00:35.714 --> 00:00:38.672
counts, and nesting in the accepted
contract.

12
00:00:38.672 --> 00:00:41.822
If depth must be controlled during
parsing, choose

13
00:00:41.822 --> 00:00:45.066
tooling that can enforce it at that stage.

14
00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:49.361
For imports, constrain total size, record
size, and processing batches.

15
00:00:49.361 --> 00:00:52.796
Apply deadlines to expensive external work
and design expensive

16
00:00:52.796 --> 00:00:55.946
transformations for cancellation or
isolation; a timer cannot

17
00:00:55.946 --> 00:00:59.572
interrupt synchronous parsing that blocks
the same event loop.

18
00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:03.198
Test each decision using both accepted and
rejected inputs.

19
00:01:03.198 --> 00:01:05.679
Cover malformed syntax, missing fields,
wrong

20
00:01:05.679 --> 00:01:07.779
types, unexpected nested properties,
oversized

21
00:01:07.779 --> 00:01:09.974
collections, invalid discriminators,
unsupported versions,

22
00:01:09.974 --> 00:01:12.551
incompatible options, and unauthorized
resource references.

23
00:01:12.551 --> 00:01:14.936
Exercise coercion with ambiguous strings,
boundary

24
00:01:14.936 --> 00:01:17.131
numbers, and rejected date
representations.

25
00:01:17.131 --> 00:01:19.803
Verify that accepted normalization reaches
the service

26
00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:22.666
and that the original body does not.

27
00:01:22.666 --> 00:01:26.579
Test resource limits at the layer that
enforces them, including

28
00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:30.205
compressed and streamed input when those
features are supported.

29
00:01:30.205 --> 00:01:34.213
Trace the full path from bytes to parsing,
runtime validation,

30
00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:38.126
the accepted object, domain checks,
authorization, and side effects.

31
00:01:38.126 --> 00:01:41.943
Rejection tests should assert that
protected writes or sends did

32
00:01:41.943 --> 00:01:45.665
not occur, not merely that an error was
returned.

33
00:01:45.665 --> 00:01:49.673
Include alternate consumers such as queue
handlers, webhook endpoints, file

34
00:01:49.673 --> 00:01:53.586
imports, configuration loaders, caches,
and less trusted database JSON.

35
00:01:53.586 --> 00:01:56.640
A shared schema helps only when every
relevant

36
00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.503
path invokes it and consumes its result.

37
00:01:59.503 --> 00:02:02.938
Review adapters and fallback branches for
routes around the

38
00:02:02.938 --> 00:02:06.660
boundary, and keep those paths covered as
contracts evolve.

